[2026-03-14T20:30:51.368Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0122RoBX9WstA5ExZpGsUiXE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The `COALESCE` approach has an issue - if description is set to an empty string `''`, `COALESCE` will use it (not null), which is correct. But if `description` is in `updates` (description !== undefined), it should always be used. The problem is the `COALESCE` logic: if `updates.description` is `''`, it's truthy for the ternary but COALESCE with `''` will return `''` which is correct.\n\nActually wait - the issue is that `COALESCE(null, description)` keeps the old value, but when description IS in updates, we want to set it even if it's empty string. The current logic sets `updates.description !== undefined ? updates.description : null` - when description is in updates, this evaluates to `updates.description` (which could be `''`), and `COALESCE('', description)` returns `''` which is correct.\n\nThe real issue is with the COALESCE approach for the case where all three could be sent in one request. Let me simplify by building the query differently to only update the fields that were provided."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":549,"cache_read_input_tokens":93517,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":549,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7c3ec87-3773-46b4-8373-fe050b22f9e3","uuid":"9fc3e6cd-b8b1-430d-893f-05fcebca9502"}
